    {
      "id": "b053e57fd10b",
      "name": "hermes-chat-sync-4h",
      "prompt": "Resolve the current local date/time with `date`. Then synchronize recent Hermes chat activity into the Obsidian vault at /var/home/rainbow/Documents/SecondBrain.\n\nFollow the `hermes-obsidian-sync` skill strictly, with these priority rules:\n1. Read the current live-sync note first: `/var/home/rainbow/Documents/SecondBrain/04 Resources/Hermes Chat Live Sync.md`.\n2. Resolve today’s daily-note path from the runtime date and read that note before making any rewrite/patch.\n3. Treat those two note files as the continuity baseline. Preserve already-curated durable state unless newer verified evidence supersedes it.\n4. Use `session_search` to browse recent sessions, but only drill into the most relevant substantive sessions from the last 24 hours, and avoid brute-reading full prior sync sessions unless a narrowed slice is truly needed.\n5. If recent results are crowded by prior cron sync runs, treat them as maintenance context rather than primary source material.\n6. If present, inspect `~/.hermes/logs/hermes-memory-capture.jsonl`, `~/.hermes/logs/hermes-obsidian-file-events.jsonl`, and `~/.hermes/state/obsidian-dirty-paths.json` for high-signal local hints, but prefer search-first and tail-window reads for large files.\n7. Never brute-read a large log or note from line 1 if it is big. If a read exceeds safety limits, immediately re-read only a small tail window near the end using the reported total_lines/size hint.\n8. Capture only durable/high-signal items: workflow changes, configuration facts, verified runtime state, note syncs, cron changes, maintenance actions, and stable follow-ups.\n9. Rewrite `/var/home/rainbow/Documents/SecondBrain/04 Resources/Hermes Chat Live Sync.md` as a curated note with frontmatter, last synced timestamp, scope, recent sessions, key durable themes, follow-ups, and privacy notes.\n10. Ensure today's daily note exists and contains or updates a `## Hermes Chat Sync` section linking to `[[Hermes Chat Live Sync]]` with 3-5 concise bullets.\n11. Keep the daily note thin: one link bullet plus only the most durable 2-4 items. Do not copy detailed PR/commit/test minutiae into the daily note unless they changed an ongoing operating rule.\n12. Keep the output curated and concise. Do NOT dump raw transcripts or secrets.\n13. Prefer rewriting the live-sync note cleanly rather than appending duplicate sections forever.\n14. Treat `~/.hermes/memories/MEMORY.md` as the canonical agent-facing durable memory store; vault `MEMORY.md` and live-sync notes are curated human-readable surfaces, while session/log/state files are retrieval or telemetry sources.\n\nVerification:\n- Read back the files you wrote/updated and confirm the sync landed.\n- If there are no meaningful new sessions, keep the note stable and only refresh the timestamp when appropriate.\n- Report only the curated result, not raw logs.",
