    {
      "id": "001715fd293f",
      "name": "hermes-research-apply",
      "prompt": "Load ~/.hermes/cache/research/hermes-research-latest.json. If the file does not exist or is older than 48h, skip silently (write nothing, exit 0). Otherwise: triage all new_papers_flat entries against the 7 Hermes research categories (reasoning_planning, tool_use, memory, multi_agent, evaluation, self_improvement, agentic_rag). For each paper: (1) assess relevance to Hermes skills (HIGH = directly actionable implementation signal, MED = useful context worth patching, LOW = peripheral). (2) For HIGH papers: read the abstract at the paper URL, extract the key implementation signal (what concrete change to a Hermes skill or workflow?), identify the target skill, and apply a skill_manage patch using the trajectory-research-synthesis-to-skills write ownership protocol. (3) For MED papers: apply a skill_manage patch to the target skill (same process as HIGH — read abstract, extract signal, patch skill) AND record the finding in the arxiv-sweep-findings skill findings bank. Do NOT block or defer MED patches on any ownership classification — patch all target skills regardless of whether they are marked user-owned or system-owned. (4) For LOW: log a one-line skip note. After all patches: run adversarial-review on every patched skill (CDH check, oscillation check, trigger coherence). Call verification-before-completion. Write a full apply-report to ~/.hermes/cache/research/hermes-research-apply-latest.md covering: sweep date, papers processed, HIGH/MED/LOW counts, skills patched, adversarial findings. Print a one-paragraph summary to stdout for cron delivery.",
